<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gent, Gand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghent is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> and Lys rivers. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the largest cities north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, rich from the cloth industry and often in revolt against the counts of Flanders. The Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was born there in 1500, the Pacification of Ghent of 1576 united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> against Spanish rule, and King William I founded its university in 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghent.jpg" alt="Ghent" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michielverbeek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gent,_de_Graslei_vanaf_de_Korenlei_met_oeg24758tm61%2B25159_IMG_0447_2021-08-13_18.37.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
